          "id": "q5",
          "question": "What is a Process Control Block (PCB)? Explain its important fields and the data structures used by an OS for process management.",
          "answer": "1. Definition\n A Process Control Block (PCB) is a data structure maintained by the Operating System to store\n important information about a process. Every process has its own PCB. The OS uses it to manage\n processes and perform context switching.\n\n 2. Important Fields of PCB\n  1. Process ID (PID): A unique identification number assigned to each process.\n\n  2. Process state: Stores the current state, such as New, Ready, Running, Waiting or Terminated.\n\n  3. Program counter: Stores the address of the next instruction to be executed.\n\n  4. CPU registers: Stores register values needed when the process resumes.\n\n  5. CPU scheduling information: Contains priority and scheduling information.\n\n  6. Memory management information: Contains information about allocated memory, such as page\n    tables and memory limits.\n\n  7. I/O status information: Stores details about allocated I/O devices and open files.\n\n  8. Accounting information: Stores CPU usage, execution time and process-owner information.\n\n 3. PCB Diagram\n                                         PROCESS CONTROL BLOCK (PCB)\n\n                                                Process ID (PID)\n\n                                                 Process State\n\n                                               Program Counter\n\n                                                 CPU Registers\n\n                                          CPU Scheduling Information\n\n                                     Memory Management Information\n\n                                             I/O Status Information\n\n                                            Accounting Information\n\n\n 4. Data Structures Used for Process Management\n  • Process table: Stores information about all processes. Each process has an associated PCB.\n\n  • Ready queue: Contains processes that are ready and waiting for CPU allocation.\n\n  • Waiting queue: Contains processes waiting for I/O operations or specific events.\n\n  • Job queue: Contains processes submitted to the system.\n\n  • Process list: Maintains information about active processes and their relationships.\n\n 5. Role of PCB in Context Switching\n Context switching means switching the CPU from one process to another.\n\n  1. The OS stops the currently running process.\n\n  2. It saves the current process information in its PCB.\n\n  3. The scheduler selects another process.\n\n  4. The OS loads the selected process information from its PCB.\n\n  5. The new process resumes execution.\n\n Conclusion: PCB is an important data structure used to store process information. It helps in process\n scheduling, context switching and process management."
