          "id": "q6",
          "question": "Compare process execution and management in Linux and Windows 11, considering process creation, execution and termination.",
          "answer": "1. Introduction\n Linux and Windows 11 are popular operating systems that support multitasking and multiple\n processes. Both manage processes, allocate CPU time and provide system services, but their internal\n methods are different.\n\n 2. Process Management in Linux\n  • Every process has a unique Process ID (PID).\n\n  • Linux uses fork() to create a new process.\n\n  • It uses exec() to replace the current process program with a new program.\n\n  • It uses wait() or waitpid() to wait for child-process completion.\n\n  • It uses exit() to terminate a process.\n\n  • Linux uses scheduling algorithms to allocate CPU time.\n\n 3. Process Management in Windows 11\n  • Every process has a unique Process ID (PID).\n\n  • Windows uses the CreateProcess() function to create a new process.\n\n  • It uses threads to execute tasks within a process.\n\n  • It provides Windows APIs to manage processes.\n\n  • It uses WaitForSingleObject() to wait for an object or process to become signaled.\n\n  • It uses ExitProcess() to terminate a process.\n\n 4. Difference Between Linux and Windows 11\n  Basis                    Linux                                 Windows 11\n\n  Type                     Open-source OS                        Proprietary OS\n\n  Process creation         fork() and exec()                     CreateProcess()\n\n  Process identification   PID                                   PID\n\n  Process execution        Uses processes and threads            Uses processes and threads\n\n  Process waiting          wait() / waitpid()                    WaitForSingleObject()\n\n  Process termination      exit()                                ExitProcess()\n\n  User interface           CLI and GUI                           Mainly GUI; also CLI\n\n  Kernel                   Linux kernel                          Windows NT kernel\n\n\n\n 5. Similarities\n  1. Both support multitasking.\n\n  2. Both manage CPU and memory resources.\n\n  3. Both use process IDs to identify processes.\n\n  4. Both support threads.\n\n  5. Both provide process creation and termination facilities.\n\n  6. Both provide protection between processes.\n\n Conclusion: Linux and Windows 11 use different mechanisms for process creation and management.\n However, both efficiently manage processes and support multitasking."
