          "id": "q7",
          "question": "What is a Real-Time Operating System (RTOS)? Differentiate between Hard Real-Time and Soft Real-Time systems with suitable applications.",
          "answer": "1. Definition\n A Real-Time Operating System (RTOS) is an operating system that gives a response within a fixed or\n specified time limit. It is designed for applications where completing a task at the correct time is very\n important.\n\n Examples: FreeRTOS, VxWorks and QNX.\n\n 2. Features of RTOS\n  1. Fast response: Responds quickly to external events.\n\n  2. Predictability: Provides predictable execution time.\n\n  3. Priority-based scheduling: Important tasks can get higher priority.\n\n  4. Multitasking: Supports execution of multiple tasks.\n\n  5. Reliability: Designed to work continuously with high dependability.\n\n  6. Low latency: Reduces the delay between an event and its response.\n\n 3. Types of Real-Time Operating Systems\n Hard Real-Time Operating System\n\n  • Tasks must complete within their specified deadlines.\n\n  • Missing a deadline can cause serious failure.\n\n  • Timing is extremely important.\n\n  • Examples: Aircraft control systems, medical life-support systems and airbag control systems.\n\n Soft Real-Time Operating System\n\n  • The system tries to complete tasks within the given deadline.\n\n  • Missing a deadline occasionally is acceptable.\n\n  • A delay may reduce performance but does not necessarily cause complete system failure.\n\n  • Examples: Video streaming, online gaming and video conferencing.\n\n 4. Difference Between Hard and Soft Real-Time Systems\n  Basis                    Hard Real-Time                       Soft Real-Time\n\n  Deadline                 Must be strictly followed            Can occasionally be missed\n\n  Failure                  Can cause serious failure            Usually reduces performance\n\n  Timing                   Very strict                          Less strict\n\n  Flexibility              Less flexible                        More flexible\n\n  Example                  Aircraft control                     Video streaming\n\n\n\n 5. Applications of RTOS\n  1. Medical systems: Patient monitoring and life-support equipment.\n\n  2. Automobiles: Airbags, braking and engine control systems.\n\n  3. Industrial automation: Robots and manufacturing machines.\n\n  4. Aerospace: Aircraft control and navigation.\n\n  5. Communication: Real-time communication devices.\n\n Conclusion: RTOS is used in systems where timing is important. Hard real-time systems must strictly\n meet deadlines, while soft real-time systems can tolerate occasional delays."
