          "id": "q9",
          "question": "Round Robin scheduling numerical",
          "answer": "Question data: P1 (AT=0, BT=5), P2 (AT=1, BT=3), P3 (AT=2, BT=1), P4 (AT=3, BT=2), P5 (AT=4, BT=3). The\n question does not specify a time quantum, so this solution assumes Time Quantum = 2 units.\n\n 1. Introduction\n Round Robin (RR) is a preemptive CPU scheduling algorithm. Each process receives a fixed amount of CPU time\n called a time quantum. If it does not finish in that time, it is placed at the end of the ready queue.\n\n 2. Gantt chart and execution order\n The ready-queue order gives the following execution sequence:\n\n          P1                    P2        P3        P1              P4                    P5        P2         P1      P5\n\n 0 2 4 5 7 9 11 12 13 14\n\n\n 3. Completion time, turnaround time, waiting time and response time\n Formulas: TAT = CT − AT; WT = TAT − BT; RT = First CPU Start − AT.\n\n  Process            AT              BT             CT              TAT                   WT              RT\n\n  P1                 0               5              13              13                    8               0\n\n  P2                 1               3              12              11                    8               1\n\n  P3                 2               1              5               3                     2               2\n\n  P4                 3               2              9               6                     4               4\n\n  P5                 4               3              14              10                    7               5\n\n  Total                                                             43                    29              12\n\n\n Average TAT = 43 ÷ 5 = 8.6 units\n Average WT = 29 ÷ 5 = 5.8 units\n Average RT = 12 ÷ 5 = 2.4 units\n Conclusion: Round Robin provides fair CPU allocation and is suitable for time-sharing systems. Its performance\n depends strongly on the selected time quantum."
