          "id": "q40",
          "question": "Explain different Disk Failure Modes. How are latent sector errors and block corruption handled? Explain the role of checksums in detecting corruption.",
          "answer": "1. Introduction\n A Disk Failure occurs when a storage device cannot correctly store, read or write data. Disk failures may result in\n data loss, corrupted files or system errors. The Operating System uses different techniques to detect and handle disk\n failures.\n\n 2. Types of Disk Failure Modes\n  1. Complete Disk Failure: The entire disk stops working. Data becomes inaccessible. It may happen due to\n    hardware damage or electronic failure.\n\n  2. Partial Disk Failure: Only a particular part of the disk stops working. Some files or sectors may become\n    inaccessible while other parts continue working.\n\n  3. Latent Sector Errors: A disk sector becomes unreadable. The error may remain undetected until the OS tries to\n    access that sector, causing a read failure.\n\n  4. Block Corruption: Stored data becomes damaged or modified incorrectly, so it no longer matches the original\n    information. It can happen due to hardware problems or interrupted write operations.\n\n  5. Transient Errors: Temporary errors during disk operations that may disappear when the operation is retried.\n\n 3. Latent Sector Errors\n A Latent Sector Error is a disk error in which a particular sector cannot be read correctly when requested.\n\n Example: Suppose a file is stored in sectors 10, 11 and 12. If Sector 11 becomes unreadable, the OS may fail to\n read the complete file.\n\n Handling latent sector errors:\n\n  1. The OS detects the error during a read operation.\n\n  2. The disk controller reports the error.\n\n  3. The OS may retry the read operation.\n\n  4. If the sector remains unreadable, an error is reported.\n\n  5. If possible, data is recovered using redundancy or a backup.\n\n  6. The faulty sector may be replaced or remapped by the storage device.\n\n 4. Block Corruption\n Block Corruption occurs when the contents of a disk block become incorrect or damaged.\n\n Example: Original data: HELLO WORLD. Corrupted data: HEXXO WORXD. The contents have changed\n unexpectedly.\n\n 5. Handling Block Corruption\n  1. Detect corrupted data using checksums.\n\n  2. Compare the stored checksum with the newly calculated checksum.\n\n  3. If the values do not match, corruption is detected.\n\n  4. Attempt to recover correct data using backups or redundant copies.\n\n  5. Report the error if recovery is not possible.\n\n 6. What is a Checksum?\n A Checksum is a value calculated from a block of data to help detect accidental data corruption. When data is stored,\n its checksum is calculated and saved. When the data is read again, a new checksum is calculated and compared\n with the original checksum. If both values match, the data has passed checksum verification. If they do not match,\n corruption is detected.\n\n 7. Working of Checksum\n  1. Original data is created.\n\n  2. The system calculates its checksum.\n\n  3. Data and checksum are stored.\n\n  4. Later, the data is read.\n\n  5. A new checksum is calculated.\n\n  6. Both checksum values are compared.\n\n  7. If they match, verification passes. Otherwise, corruption is reported.\n\n     Checksum Verification\n\n     Original Data → Calculate Checksum → Store Data + Checksum\n\n     ↓\n\n     Read Data → Calculate New Checksum\n\n     ↓\n\n     Compare Both Checksums\n\n     Match: Verification Passed | Mismatch: Corruption Detected\n\n\n 8. Advantages of Checksums\n  • Helps detect data corruption.\n\n  • Improves data reliability.\n\n  • Helps identify damaged blocks.\n\n  • Supports data integrity checking.\n\n  • Helps prevent undetected corruption.\n\n 9. Latent Sector Error vs Block Corruption\n  Basis                   Latent Sector Error                           Block Corruption\n\n  Meaning                 Sector becomes unreadable                     Stored data becomes incorrect\n\n  Main problem            Read failure                                  Data integrity failure\n\n  Detection               During sector access                          Through integrity checking\n\n  Solution                Retry, remap or recover                       Checksum verification and recovery\n\n  Result                  Data may become inaccessible                  Data may be incorrect\n\n\n Conclusion: Disk failures can cause data loss and corruption. Latent sector errors make sectors unreadable, while\n block corruption damages stored information. Checksums help detect corruption and improve data reliability.\n\n Use this table to revise the key ideas from all eight answers before the examination.\n\n  Q.       Topic                             Important concepts\n\n  33       I/O Architecture                  Status, command and data registers; canonical protocol\n\n  34       Interrupts and DMA                Polling, ISR, DMA controller\n\n  35       Device Drivers                    Driver architecture, IDE disk driver\n\n  36       Hard Disk Drives                  Platters, tracks, sectors, seek time, latency\n\n  37       Files and Directories             File attributes, directory structures, file operations\n\n  38       File System Implementation        Superblock, inode, indexing, free-space management\n\n  39       Fast File System                  Cylinder groups, locality, block allocation\n\n  40       Disk Failure Modes                Latent sector errors, block corruption, checksums\n\n\n\n Important formulas\n Total Disk I/O Time = Seek Time + Rotational Latency + Transfer Time\n\n Average Rotational Latency = Time for one rotation ÷ 2\n\n Rotation Time (seconds) = 60 ÷ RPM\n\n Diagrams worth practising\n  • I/O system architecture and canonical device.\n\n  • DMA data-transfer diagram.\n\n  • Device-driver architecture and IDE driver flow.\n\n  • Hard disk geometry: platter, track, sector, cylinder and head.\n\n  • Tree-structured directory.\n\n  • Inode and multi-level indexing.\n\n  • FFS cylinder groups.\n\n  • Checksum verification flow.\n\n Exam Preparation Tips\n  1. Practise numerical problems from CPU scheduling and disk I/O time.\n\n  2. Revise paging, segmentation and TLB concepts from memory management.\n\n  3. Memorise semaphore algorithms from concurrency.\n\n  4. Practise the DMA, HDD geometry and inode diagrams.\n\n  5. Revise definitions, advantages and comparison tables from every unit.\n\n Note: This PDF contains the eight Unit 5 answers in the agreed answer bank. It is a study and revision aid, not a\n guarantee of the exact questions that will appear in the examination."
